# Public Servers
Servers other people run, each read through the Spell `spell place ingest` wrote for it, untouched:  each a page of its own, on a tab, in one centred column.
The tab chosen, and each page's search, are kept in the page's address (`?server=met&art=van+gogh`), so Back steps through them.
GitHub's is first, then Open Library's, then JSONPlaceholder's, which takes writes, then Open-Meteo's, the weather, then the Met Museum's artworks, then REST Countries', every country.

<spell-app "public servers">
	<spell>
		- its "tab" is text
		- its "github" is a new github page
		- its "library" is a new open library page
		- its "weather" is a new open meteo page
		- its "museum" is a new met museum page
		- its "atlas" is a new rest countries page
		- to create it:
			set its tab to "github"
	</spell>

	<draw>
		<container>
			<stack spacing="lg">
				<form value={it}>
					<tabs name="tab" parameter="server" appearance="secondary pointing basic" aria-label="Servers">
						<tab label="GitHub" value="github" />
						<tab label="Open Library" value="library" />
						<tab label="JSONPlaceholder" value="jsonplaceholder" />
						<tab label="Open-Meteo" value="weather" />
						<tab label="The Met" value="met" />
						<tab label="REST Countries" value="countries" />
					</tabs>
				</form>
				{draw the server of it}
			</stack>
		</container>
	</draw>

	<draw "server" description="The tab's page:  its own drawing, so its `if` picks just one, and only that one asks its server">
		<if its tab is "github">
			draw its github
		<otherwise if its tab is "library">
			draw its library
		<otherwise if its tab is "jsonplaceholder">
			draw (a new jsonplaceholder page)
		<otherwise if its tab is "weather">
			draw its weather
		<otherwise if its tab is "met">
			draw its museum
		<otherwise if its tab is "countries">
			draw its atlas
		</>
	</draw>
</spell-app>
